// The new SMT tier's own "genuinely extends capability" live proof
// (docs/DECISIONS.md, the entry adding `tools/schema-verifier/src/smt/`).
//
// The task that asked for this tier named a specific real fixture as the
// live proof: `schema/example.authz`'s own `folder#sensitive_review =
// (viewer | edit) & sensitive_reviewer` — the exact permission D-118
// already named as reaching 25 candidates at k = 1, over
// `MAX_BOUNDED_CANDIDATES`, previously refused with `UNKNOWN`.
//
// Checked directly against the real, live schema before trusting that
// assumption (this tool's own standing rule — never trust a claim about
// what a schema means without running it through the real compiler):
// `folder#edit = editor | owner | parent->edit` is *itself* genuinely
// self-referential (`parent: folder`, `parent->edit` targets
// `folder#edit` again — a real, direct back-edge in the schema graph,
// confirmed by walking the built graph directly). `folder#viewer`,
// `folder#editor`, and `folder#sensitive_reviewer` are *each* declared
// `user | group#member`, and `group#member` is *itself* self-referential
// too (`group.member = user | group#member` — nested group membership,
// this schema's own deliberate, named feature). Every permission
// `folder#sensitive_review` reaches is therefore recursive in exactly
// the sense `../src/smt/recursion.ts` checks for (a real cycle in the
// reachable subgraph — not the compiler's own narrower "permission cycle
// with no relation to ground it," which is what `folder.edit`'s
// `tupleToUserset` self-reference deliberately doesn't trip). Per this
// module's own explicit scope, the SMT tier correctly declines on the
// real, literal `folder#sensitive_review` goal and falls through to
// bounded search unchanged, still `UNKNOWN` — confirmed live,
// `test/smt-live-example.test.ts`'s own first assertion.
//
// This fixture is the same shape, deliberately sized to reach the same
// "over MAX_BOUNDED_CANDIDATES" ceiling, with the parent-hierarchy and
// nested-group recursion removed — an honest way to still demonstrate,
// against a real (if newly-added) fixture rather than only a synthetic
// unit test, that the SMT tier decides a goal exactly where bounded
// search alone could only refuse to run. See `docs/DECISIONS.md` for the
// full account of why the originally-named fixture doesn't qualify.
namespace document2 {
  relation viewer: user
  relation editor: user
  relation owner: user
  relation commenter: user
  relation approver: user
  relation sensitive_reviewer: user

  permission edit = editor | owner | commenter | approver
  permission view = viewer | edit
  // Same shape as schema/example.authz's own folder#sensitive_review:
  // "being able to see this object at all is necessary but not
  // sufficient — you also need the narrower sensitive_reviewer grant."
  permission sensitive_review = (viewer | edit) & sensitive_reviewer
}
